import {concept as c, project as p, q} from './framework.js';

// Two further concepts per unit. All scenarios and assessment passages are original.
export default [
c('kairos','Kairos & timing',1,'situation','1.A 2.A',
'Kairos concerns the fitness of a response to a particular moment and setting. A writer considers when an audience can listen, what decision remains open, and which circumstances make an action possible.',
'Distinguish the event that prompts speech from the opportunity to influence it. Urgency needs a reason: identify what changes if the audience acts later. A timely appeal can still rely on poor evidence.',
'A request to include shaded seating in a park renovation has different force before the design is approved than after construction begins.',
'Kairos is not simply mentioning a date or insisting that an issue is urgent. Explain why the timing changes the audience’s available choices.','exigence-context audience',
p('The decision window','Design an argument whose timing changes what its audience can do.',
'Choose a school decision with three stages: consultation, approval, and implementation. Write a 350–500 word appeal at each stage using the same factual dossier. Specify who can still change what; label any invented details.',
'Build a folding calendar with movable text panels. Use space and placement to show when each option opens or closes; provide a linear text version.',
'Compare six choices across the appeals. Explain how timing changes the grounds for action, the opening, and the scale of the request.',
'Defend the most useful intervention point using the decision process as evidence. Address an advantage of acting later; revise one appeal after testing whether a reader understands the deadline.',
'When did your sense of urgency become an unsupported pressure tactic, and how did you correct it?'),
{passage:'Original fictional community letter: “The park committee will approve a renovation plan on Friday. Once the paving contract is signed, adding shade will require a second excavation. Residents have asked for cooler seating for years; this week, that familiar request can still become part of the original design. Ask the committee to compare shaded and unshaded layouts before it votes. We need not pretend that one meeting will solve every access problem to recognize what this meeting can decide.”',
read:q('The reference to a second excavation primarily serves to',[
['explain why making the request before approval could prevent avoidable work.','The contract creates a decision window: later changes require repeating part of the work.'],
['establish that the committee has deliberately ignored residents.','A history of requests does not establish the committee’s motives.'],
['demonstrate that shaded seating is inexpensive to maintain.','The passage concerns installation timing, not maintenance expenses.'],
['suggest that the existing design has already been approved.','The Friday vote is still in the future.']],'1.A'),
write:q('The writer wants an opening that emphasizes the audience’s present opportunity to act. Which sentence best replaces the first sentence?',[
['Before Friday’s design vote, residents can ask the committee to include shade in the initial renovation.','This opening connects a deadline, an audience action, and the decision still available.'],
['For generations, public parks have served as gathering places for residents.','The broad history does not identify this decision window.'],
['The park committee has scheduled another meeting to discuss several matters.','This wording obscures both the impending vote and the relevant choice.'],
['Future residents will judge the park committee by the beauty of its renovation.','The prediction shifts attention to reputation without explaining what residents can do now.']],'2.A')},
{type:'analysis',packet:'gate',focus:'Explain how the impending print deadline and unresolved access problem shape the speaker’s request.'}),

c('genre-conventions','Genre & conventions',1,'situation','1.A 2.B',
'A genre is a recurring kind of communication shaped by what a community uses it to accomplish. Conventions of structure, evidence, tone, and presentation help readers recognize how to use a text.',
'Compare actual examples before treating a convention as a rule. Ask what work a familiar feature performs and what readers would need if you changed it. Genre can guide invention without determining every choice.',
'A closure notice helps visitors plan a route; an editorial can argue about whether the closure is justified. Both concern the same event, but they invite different kinds of reading.',
'Genre is more than a label such as “essay” or a fixed format. Its conventions respond to purpose, readers, and circumstances.','purpose-message audience',
p('One event, three documents','Translate a public decision across genres without changing the facts.',
'Create a shared dossier for a fictional museum closure. Compose a 120-word visitor notice, a 450-word editorial, and a 350-word internal recommendation. Each must answer the questions its readers need answered.',
'Design a document wall with three distinct reading paths. Annotate hierarchy, placement, and format; offer an accessible plain-text sequence.',
'Use six excerpts to explain how genre changes selection and ordering of evidence. Identify one convention you deliberately break and assess the cost.',
'Argue which document should lead public communication. Defend your choice against another genre’s strengths, then revise the notice after a reader tries to find the closure dates and alternative access.',
'Which apparent style preference turned out to be necessary for a reader to use the document?'),
{passage:'Original fictional notice and editorial: “NOTICE: The east entrance closes May 3–10 for ramp repairs. Use the west entrance on Cedar Street; a step-free route remains open.” A student editorial about the same closure begins, “A repair can improve access eventually while making it harder today. The museum should publish the temporary route wherever it advertises the exhibition.” Both texts address visitors, but they give the closure different rhetorical roles.',
read:q('Which distinction between the two texts is best supported by their wording?',[
['The notice supplies immediate directions, while the editorial uses the closure to justify a communication policy.','The notice identifies dates and a route; the editorial argues for publishing that route more widely.'],
['The notice evaluates the repairs, while the editorial merely reports their schedule.','The notice does not evaluate the repairs, and the editorial does not supply their dates.'],
['The editorial disputes the closure dates given in the notice.','The editorial challenges communication, not the accuracy of the dates.'],
['The notice justifies the repair decision, while the editorial explains how to reach the entrance.','This reverses their functions: the notice supplies directions, while the editorial argues for a communication change.']],'1.A'),
write:q('The writer is revising the notice to help first-time visitors use the temporary entrance. Which addition best fits that purpose and genre?',[
['The west entrance is beside the Cedar Street bus stop, 80 metres north of the main gate.','A location landmark and distance make the practical directions more usable.'],
['The museum has long been an important site of civic conversation.','Institutional history does not help a visitor locate the entrance.'],
['Temporary closures raise questions about the meaning of public access.','The abstract question belongs to a broader argument and does not resolve the immediate need.'],
['The editorial board welcomes further discussion of the repair policy.','An invitation to debate does not supply the missing route information.']],'2.B')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'Public institutions often communicate through familiar forms such as notices, reports, and open letters. Write an essay that argues your position on the extent to which writers should follow a genre’s conventions when communicating an unfamiliar problem.',focus:'Use specific examples to weigh reader expectations against the need to adapt a form.'}),

c('fact-value-policy','Claims of fact, value & policy',2,'evidence','3.A 4.B',
'Claims of fact ask what is true, claims of value evaluate using a standard, and claims of policy recommend action. A single argument may combine all three, but each step needs its own support.',
'Track the move from a finding to a judgment and from that judgment to a proposal. A true description does not settle what matters most or which intervention is best. Make the evaluative standard visible.',
'“The last bus leaves at six” describes a schedule. “That schedule is inadequate for evening workers” applies an access standard. “Add a seven o’clock bus” recommends a policy.',
'A policy claim is not proved merely by showing that a problem exists; alternatives, consequences, and feasibility still matter.','claims evidence logos',
p('The three-claim hearing','Build a proposal whose factual, evaluative, and practical steps can withstand separate challenges.',
'Prepare three 350-word hearing briefs on a school resource: what is happening, how it should be judged, and what should change. Use at least three traceable sources or a clearly labeled common fictional dossier.',
'Create a three-level evidence board with different connectors for observation, value judgment, and proposed action. Make each connector’s reasoning available in text.',
'Annotate where the briefs change claim type. Explain how six wording and organizational choices help readers recognize the standard being applied.',
'Write a final recommendation that answers an objection to your chosen standard and an objection to your proposed remedy. Revise the weakest inferential link.',
'Which fact remained uncontested even when your audience rejected the policy, and what explains that disagreement?'),
{passage:'Original fictional committee draft: “The computer lab closes at four, although the late activity bus leaves at five. Students waiting for that bus have no supervised place to finish digital assignments. A school that assigns online work should provide a reasonable chance to complete it on campus. The lab should remain open until the late bus departs, with staffing costs reviewed after a six-week trial.”',
read:q('The statement beginning “A school that assigns online work” primarily provides',[
['a standard that connects the described access gap to the proposed change.','It establishes a responsibility against which the current schedule can be judged.'],
['a measurement of how many students use the lab.','The statement gives a value-based standard, not attendance data.'],
['evidence that the proposed staffing arrangement is affordable.','Affordability remains to be examined during the proposed review.'],
['a concession that the school cannot affect students’ access.','The statement instead assigns the school a responsibility to provide access.']],'3.A'),
write:q('Which revision of the final sentence best develops a defensible policy thesis while preserving the writer’s concern about cost?',[
['The school should test supervised lab access until five for six weeks and use attendance and staffing costs to decide whether to continue.','The proposal states an action and specifies criteria for evaluating it.'],
['The school’s computer lab closes before the late activity bus departs.','This reports a fact without proposing a course of action.'],
['Digital assignments are an important part of contemporary education.','The general evaluation does not address the access gap or the cost concern.'],
['The lab must stay open until five permanently because all students will use it.','This makes an unsupported prediction and removes the cost-sensitive trial.']],'4.B')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'Schools and public institutions often justify a new policy by identifying an existing problem. Write an essay that argues your position on what a proposal must establish beyond the existence of a problem before an institution should adopt it.',focus:'Distinguish evidence of a problem, standards of judgment, and support for a particular remedy.'}),

c('relevance-sufficiency','Relevance & sufficiency of evidence',2,'evidence','3.A 4.A',
'Relevant evidence bears on the claim being made. Sufficient evidence supports that claim’s scope and certainty. A vivid example may establish that something can happen while leaving its frequency or typicality unresolved.',
'State the exact claim before judging the evidence. Examine population, time span, comparison, and exceptions. More examples of the same narrow kind do not automatically repair a gap in coverage.',
'One student’s missed bus can show that a schedule problem is possible. A claim that most students miss the bus needs broader records and a defined group.',
'Evidence is not sufficient simply because it is detailed, credible, or numerous. Its reach must match the conclusion.','evidence logos',
p('The claim telescope','Curate evidence for three claims of increasing scope.',
'Choose a claim about a school space or routine. Write one paragraph showing possibility, one estimating a pattern, and one recommending a response. Use a small documented observation set or a labeled simulated dataset, and identify what each cannot establish.',
'Build a paper telescope or nested-frame diagram with lenses for one case, a group, and a broader population. Attach evidence to the narrowest view it supports, and mark what remains outside each view. Include a text inventory.',
'Explain six decisions about selection, placement, and qualification. Identify a striking example you excluded because it did not support the claim.',
'Defend the broadest claim your evidence warrants. Answer a proposed broader conclusion, then revise either the claim or the evidence plan rather than merely adding emphatic language.',
'Where did accumulating more of the same evidence stop improving your argument?'),
{passage:'Original fictional survey memo: “Twelve members of the robotics club answered a survey after their evening meeting. Ten said the library should stay open later. These responses show that late access matters to some students whose activities end after the library closes. They do not show what most students want: club members were surveyed together, and students without after-school activities were not included.”',
read:q('The final sentence primarily limits which possible inference from the survey?',[
['That the club responses represent the preferences of the student body as a whole.','The sentence identifies missing groups and a narrow sampling setting.'],
['That any robotics club member wants later library hours.','Ten responses directly support that narrower observation.'],
['That the survey was conducted after an evening meeting.','The timing is stated as a fact and is not challenged.'],
['That a later closing time could matter to students with activities.','The memo explicitly supports this possibility.']],'3.A'),
write:q('The writer wants to support the claim that interest in later hours extends beyond students in after-school clubs. Which additional evidence would be most relevant?',[
['Responses from a documented sample of students who do not join after-school clubs, including their reasons for wanting later access.','This examines the specific group missing from the original evidence.'],
['A second survey of the same robotics club during the next meeting.','Repeated club responses do not address the excluded group.'],
['A detailed description of the robotics club’s busiest competition week.','The description adds detail about the same narrow population.'],
['A list of awards earned by the students who completed the original survey.','Academic or club achievements do not establish other students’ preferences.']],'4.A')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'A single vivid example can make a public problem visible, but decisions often require a broader evidentiary base. Write an essay that argues your position on the role an individual example should play in justifying institutional change.',focus:'Explain what your examples establish and where additional evidence would be needed.'}),

c('enthymeme','Enthymeme & unstated premises',3,'reasoning','5.A 6.A',
'An enthymeme is an argument that leaves part of its reasoning unstated for the audience to supply. Recovering that premise reveals both the argument’s efficiency and a possible point of disagreement.',
'Reconstruct only the assumption needed to connect the given reason to the conclusion. Then ask whether this audience would accept it and whether its limits matter. An unstated premise is not automatically a flaw.',
'“Students pay the activity fee, so they should help choose the activities” relies on a premise linking financial contribution to a voice in decisions.',
'Do not invent a hidden motive when the task is to recover a premise. A premise is a proposition the inference needs, not a guess about the writer’s character.','warrants induction-deduction',
p('The missing bridge workshop','Make the invisible reasoning in a persuasive message visible.',
'Write two 450-word proposals for the same school policy, one for readers who share your central premise and one for readers who question it. Include a short reconstruction of each argument as reasons, premise, and conclusion.',
'Create a bridge model or flat diagram with removable premise spans. Label the consequences when a span is removed; supply a text reconstruction.',
'Quote six moments of compression or explanation. Explain which audience knowledge permits an omission and where the omission risks misreading.',
'Defend your central premise with an example and a difficult exception. Revise the proposal so that the exception changes its actual scope.',
'Which omitted idea seemed obvious only because you shared it with your first audience?'),
{passage:'Original fictional editorial: “Students pay an activity fee each term. They should therefore have representatives on the committee that allocates it. Representatives need not decide every purchase; staff still hold responsibility for safety and accounts. But consulting students only after the budget is settled gives contributors no meaningful influence over the activities their money supports.”',
read:q('The reasoning of the first two sentences depends most directly on which unstated premise?',[
['People who contribute to a shared fund should have some influence over its allocation.','This premise connects paying the fee to representation on the allocating committee.'],
['Students have more accounting expertise than staff members.','The editorial preserves staff responsibility for accounts and does not compare expertise.'],
['Every activity receives the same amount of funding.','Equal allocations are not necessary for the representation claim.'],
['Consultation is useful only when contributors make every final decision.','The passage explicitly permits a limited representative role.']],'5.A'),
write:q('The writer wants to explain why representation would improve this particular decision process. Which addition best develops that reasoning?',[
['Representatives could identify unmet activity needs before allocations become difficult to revise.','The sentence explains a practical contribution at the point when decisions remain changeable.'],
['The committee’s allocation meetings occur once every term.','The schedule alone does not explain what representation contributes.'],
['Students are the people who pay the activity fee.','This repeats the original premise without developing its significance.'],
['A committee with more members will necessarily reach better decisions.','The categorical claim substitutes an unsupported rule for a mechanism.']],'6.A')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'Shared assumptions can help a community act efficiently, yet those assumptions may exclude people who do not share them. Write an essay that argues your position on when a public argument should make its underlying assumptions explicit.',focus:'Develop a criterion for deciding which assumptions need explanation.'}),

c('problem-solution','Problem–solution reasoning',3,'reasoning','5.C 6.C',
'Problem–solution development connects an account of a problem to a proposed response. It must show how the response addresses the identified cause or barrier, using criteria that allow alternatives and tradeoffs to be assessed.',
'Separate the visible symptom from the mechanism producing it. A solution can be partial or experimental, but it needs a plausible link to the problem. State who acts, what changes, and how success will be judged.',
'If students miss an office because its hours overlap all their classes, a clearer sign may not solve the scheduling barrier. A different opening time might.',
'Describing a serious problem does not establish that the writer’s preferred remedy will work or that it is the only reasonable remedy.','cause-effect line-reasoning',
p('The repair manual for a system','Design competing repairs for a recurring institutional obstacle.',
'Document a low-stakes school process such as equipment checkout. Write a 450-word problem diagnosis and two 350-word proposals addressing different possible causes. Use observations or a labeled simulated case, not invented real-world results.',
'Make an exploded diagram of the process with two replaceable repair layers. Show where each proposal changes the system and who bears its costs.',
'Analyze how six textual or visual choices establish causation, allocate responsibility, or make a proposal seem feasible. Test a version that foregrounds another cause.',
'Recommend a limited pilot using explicit success and stopping criteria. Answer the strongest alternative diagnosis, then revise the proposal after a peer traces the process.',
'What evidence would persuade you that your repair targets a symptom rather than the cause?'),
{passage:'Original fictional process review: “Students line up to borrow cameras while a volunteer searches a paper log for overdue equipment. Buying another camera may shorten some waits, but it will not speed that search. A shared checkout list could make current loans visible before a student reaches the desk. Test the list for two weeks, recording search time and the number of students who leave without equipment; use those results before deciding whether inventory must also grow.”',
read:q('The passage’s discussion of buying another camera primarily',[
['distinguishes an inventory remedy from a response to the identified information bottleneck.','The writer grants a possible benefit but explains that the log search is a separate cause of delay.'],
['establishes that the existing inventory is sufficient for all demand.','The final sentence leaves open the possibility that inventory must grow.'],
['shifts the problem from student access to the expense of equipment.','No cost comparison is supplied; access and waiting remain central.'],
['argues that record keeping matters more than whether students obtain cameras.','The proposed evaluation includes students who leave without equipment.']],'5.C'),
write:q('The writer wants to evaluate an alternative solution using the same problem diagnosis. Which addition best serves that purpose?',[
['Assigning a second volunteer to find records could also reduce search delays; compare its staffing demands with those of the shared list.','The alternative addresses the same bottleneck and introduces a relevant comparison criterion.'],
['A newer camera model would produce sharper photographs for student projects.','Image quality does not address the identified search delay.'],
['Students should appreciate that the existing volunteers work hard.','The appeal does not evaluate a solution to the process problem.'],
['The shared list will eliminate every reason a student might wait.','The absolute prediction ignores other possible causes of waiting.']],'6.C')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'Institutions sometimes adopt an appealing solution before agreeing on the cause of a problem. Write an essay that argues your position on the value and limitations of testing a small intervention before committing to a larger reform.',focus:'Connect the design of a trial to what it can and cannot reveal.'}),
c('close-reading','Close reading & inference',4,'reasoning','5.A 6.A',
'Close reading builds an interpretation from specific textual relationships: wording, sequence, emphasis, and what a passage leaves unresolved. An inference moves beyond what is directly stated while remaining accountable to those details.',
'Separate observation, inference, and speculation. Test your interpretation against a detail that could challenge it. Explain the path from language to meaning rather than replacing evidence with a description of the writer’s intentions.',
'If a speaker repeats “for now” while endorsing a policy, the phrase may qualify the endorsement. A claim of secret hostility would require additional evidence.',
'A possible interpretation is not necessarily a well-supported one. Close reading does not give every interpretation equal evidentiary weight.','rhetorical-analysis commentary',
p('The annotation hearing','Put two plausible readings of one passage on trial.',
'Choose a short public-domain text or use an original passage in this library. Write two 400-word interpretations that make different claims about the same choice, quoting precise details. Keep an observation log separate from your inferences.',
'Create a transparent overlay or two-column evidence map showing which details support both readings and which distinguish them. Provide a sequential text version.',
'Explain six annotation decisions, including one omission. Identify how selection or arrangement makes one interpretation appear stronger.',
'Defend the better-supported reading while preserving a real ambiguity. Answer the rival reading’s strongest evidence, then revise one inference after a reader traces it back to the passage.',
'Which interpretation changed when you stopped treating a plausible motive as a textual fact?'),
{passage:'Original fictional speech excerpt: “We will keep the Saturday workshop, for now. The sign-up sheet records enthusiasm, but a signature cannot tell us whether someone can afford the bus fare. Before we call the empty chairs a lack of interest, let us ask who could reach the room. After that inquiry, we can decide whether to change the workshop or the route to it.”',
read:q('Which interpretation of the speaker’s position is best supported by the passage?',[
['The speaker supports continuing the workshop provisionally while investigating an alternative explanation for low attendance.','“For now” limits the commitment, and the fare question introduces an access explanation.'],
['The speaker believes sign-up sheets have no value in planning a workshop.','The passage limits what signatures establish without rejecting every use of them.'],
['The speaker has established that bus fares are the sole cause of empty chairs.','The speaker proposes an inquiry rather than announcing a proven cause.'],
['The speaker intends to cancel the workshop regardless of the inquiry’s findings.','The final sentence leaves the decision open to the inquiry’s results.']],'5.A'),
write:q('A student argues that the speaker delays judgment to improve the decision. Which commentary best explains evidence for that claim?',[
['By distinguishing willingness to sign up from ability to arrive, the speaker shows why attendance alone cannot yet justify cancellation.','This connects two textual details to the reasoning behind delaying the decision.'],
['The speaker uses the words “sign-up sheet,” which makes the speech concrete.','This observation does not explain how the detail supports the claim about judgment.'],
['The speaker probably disliked the original workshop organizers.','The speculation about motive is unsupported by the passage.'],
['The passage contains a discussion of transportation and workshops.','This summarizes the topic without explaining the argumentative relationship.']],'6.A')},
{type:'analysis',packet:'gate',focus:'Test your interpretation of the speaker’s attitude against both the acknowledgment of staff effort and the criticism of the notice.'}),

c('rhetorical-shifts','Rhetorical shifts',4,'reasoning','5.B 6.B',
'A rhetorical shift changes a text’s strategy, focus, tone, scale, or relationship to its audience. Its importance lies in how the movement changes the argument’s work, not simply in where a new paragraph begins.',
'Describe the movement with two precise terms: from a personal observation to a public criterion, or from concession to a bounded proposal. Locate a verbal or structural cue and explain why the new move follows the earlier one.',
'A writer may begin with an admired family object, then shift to the labor required to repair it, turning nostalgia into an argument about practical responsibility.',
'A transition word can signal a shift, but it does not explain the shift’s function. Some consequential shifts occur without an explicit transition.','rhetorical-analysis narrative-description',
p('The hinge exhibition','Make the turning point of an argument visible and test an alternative order.',
'Write a 600-word public address that moves from a personal scene to an institutional proposal. Produce a second version with the same evidence but a different turning point. Label fictional scenes.',
'Build a hinged poster or a sequence of panels that physically turns at the argumentative shift. Include a text version explaining the sequence and the hinge.',
'Quote the sentences before and after each turning point. Analyze six choices that prepare readers for, or resist, the change in scale and purpose.',
'Defend one version’s sequence for a named audience. Explain what the alternative version makes easier to understand, then revise the weaker transition after a reader outlines both versions.',
'Did your shift change the argument’s reasoning or only announce a new topic?'),
{passage:'Original fictional essay excerpt: “My grandfather’s repaired kettle sat in the kitchen for thirty years. Its mismatched handle made it look less like a product than a history. Yet affection cannot hold a handle in place. Someone had to know which screw would fit, and somewhere had to sell it. If we want repair to become ordinary again, we must discuss parts and training as well as memories.”',
read:q('The sentence “Yet affection cannot hold a handle in place” marks a shift from',[
['an account of an object’s emotional significance to the practical conditions that make repair possible.','The next sentences identify skills and parts, turning memory toward material requirements.'],
['a defense of repair to a recommendation that old objects be replaced.','The passage continues to support repair while explaining its requirements.'],
['a criticism of the grandfather to praise for commercial manufacturers.','Neither criticism of the grandfather nor praise for manufacturers appears.'],
['a comparison of repair techniques to an unrelated family anecdote.','The family anecdote comes first and remains connected to the argument.']],'5.B'),
write:q('The writer wants a final sentence that connects the personal example to a public proposal. Which choice best achieves that goal?',[
['A town repair program should therefore fund access to parts and instruction, giving more families the means to keep valued objects in use.','The sentence links practical conditions to a proposal while retaining the example’s personal stakes.'],
['My grandfather’s kettle was blue, with a dark patch near the spout.','The detail returns to description without advancing the public proposal.'],
['Many people remember objects from their childhood kitchens.','The generalization extends nostalgia but does not connect it to action.'],
['Training matters, and another subject worth considering is the design of modern kitchens.','This introduces a new topic instead of completing the existing movement.']],'6.B')},
{type:'analysis',packet:'repair',focus:'Explain how movements between remembered objects, practical obstacles, and public responsibilities develop the essay’s argument.'}),

c('cumulative-periodic','Cumulative & periodic sentences',5,'style','7.B 8.B',
'A cumulative sentence presents a main clause and then adds modifying detail. A periodic sentence delays its main clause or grammatical completion until after preparatory material. The placement changes how a reader encounters the relationship among ideas.',
'Mark the main clause before naming the pattern. Then explain what the reader learns immediately and what must be held in suspense. Neither pattern has an automatic emotional effect; context determines whether delay feels clarifying, solemn, or cumbersome.',
'“We opened the hall, carrying chairs from three classrooms” gives the action first. “After the chairs arrived and the doors were cleared, we opened the hall” prepares conditions before the action.',
'A long sentence is not necessarily periodic, and a short sentence can still delay its main clause. Length and grammatical arrangement are different features.','syntax coherence',
p('The opening-night reveal','Compose two versions of a public message with different patterns of arrival.',
'Write a 500-word opening address for a community exhibition. Revise six sentences into paired cumulative and periodic forms while preserving their claims. Produce a second version incorporating your strongest alternatives.',
'Build a miniature paper stage or a flat reveal sequence. Place the main clause before or behind movable panels containing its modifiers, so readers encounter each sentence in order. Include full sentence text and explain how the reveal changes emphasis.',
'Analyze all six pairs in context. Explain where delaying the main clause changes emphasis or increases memory demands; avoid assigning a fixed effect to a pattern.',
'Defend which version better serves listeners hearing the message once. Test it aloud or with a peer, then revise two sentences based on what listeners can reconstruct.',
'Where did a sentence that looked impressive on the page make the relationship among ideas harder to hear?'),
{passage:'Original fictional opening address: “After the volunteers carried the tables upstairs, after the neighbors brought photographs from drawers and wallets, after the oldest resident found a chair near the window, the exhibition finally opened. The room filled slowly, with stories crossing the spaces between display cases. What looked like a collection of objects became a gathering of people able to explain them.”',
read:q('The arrangement of the first sentence chiefly emphasizes',[
['the collective preparations that precede the opening by withholding the main event until the end.','The repeated introductory clauses delay “the exhibition finally opened” and foreground its enabling work.'],
['the opening as an immediate fact followed by optional background details.','The main event is delayed rather than stated first.'],
['a conflict between volunteers and residents over who owns the objects.','The sentence describes coordinated preparation, not a dispute.'],
['the inability of chronological sequence to explain the event.','The sequence helps establish the preparations on which the opening depends.']],'7.B'),
write:q('The writer wants to state the main event first and then accumulate details of participation. Which revision best accomplishes this goal?',[
['The exhibition finally opened, its tables carried upstairs by volunteers, its photographs brought by neighbors, its oldest visitor seated near the window.','The main clause arrives first, followed by details modifying the event.'],
['After the tables and photographs arrived, the exhibition finally opened.','The main clause remains delayed and much of the participation is removed.'],
['Although the exhibition finally opened, the oldest visitor sat near the window.','This creates a concessive relationship not supported by the original.'],
['Volunteers carried tables upstairs because the exhibition had already opened.','This reverses the timing and changes the causal relationship.']],'8.B')},
{type:'analysis',packet:'gate',focus:'Examine how the ordering of clauses controls what the audience must consider before accepting the speaker’s proposal.'}),

c('cohesion-reference','Cohesion & reference',5,'style','7.C 8.C',
'Cohesion connects sentences through recurring terms, pronouns, demonstratives, and other explicit links. Reference is clear when a reader can identify what an expression such as “they” or “this change” points to.',
'Trace each reference to a specific noun or idea. A summary noun can carry reasoning forward: “this delay” names a consequence, while “this disagreement” names a relationship. Repetition can provide precision when synonyms would blur the subject.',
'After describing an office that closes before workers arrive, “this scheduling barrier” identifies the problem more clearly than an isolated “this.”',
'Clear reference helps readers follow an argument but cannot make disconnected claims logically coherent. A pronoun may be grammatically possible and still be ambiguous.','coherence syntax',
p('The trail of this','Build and repair a reader’s path through a complex explanation.',
'Write a 500-word explanation of a school process involving at least three groups. Create a second version that improves unclear references without simplifying away important relationships. Preserve both drafts.',
'Map nouns and referring expressions as a trail with labeled branches. Distinguish deliberate repetition from ambiguity; provide a sentence-by-sentence text equivalent.',
'Analyze six revisions, explaining not only what each expression refers to but what interpretation the earlier wording invited.',
'Defend a principle for balancing concision with explicit repetition. Test two ambiguous sentences with readers and revise them using the responses as limited evidence.',
'When did replacing a repeated noun with a more elegant phrase make the argument less precise?'),
{passage:'Original fictional report: “The student editors asked the librarians to extend the archive’s opening hours. They said the current schedule prevented research after class. The librarians proposed a two-week trial with volunteers. This would give the editors time to gather evidence about demand before requesting a permanent change.” The writer intends the second sentence to report the editors’ concern.',
read:q('Why might the pronoun “They” in the second sentence impede understanding?',[
['Both the editors and the librarians are plausible plural referents, so the source of the concern is uncertain.','The previous sentence supplies two groups, and the pronoun does not specify which spoke.'],
['The pronoun disagrees in number with every noun in the preceding sentence.','Both “editors” and “librarians” are plural.'],
['The pronoun establishes that the librarians rejected the request.','It does not establish rejection; its referent is ambiguous.'],
['The pronoun makes the description shift from past to future tense.','The tense remains past in “said” and “prevented.”']],'7.C'),
write:q('Which revision of the second sentence most clearly expresses the writer’s intended meaning?',[
['The editors explained that the current schedule prevented their research after class.','The revision names the intended speakers and ties the consequence to their work.'],
['They explained to them that the current schedule prevented research after class.','The two pronouns preserve or increase uncertainty about the groups.'],
['The librarians explained that the editors preferred researching during class.','The revision changes both the speaker and the substance of the concern.'],
['This prevented research after class, as they said.','Both the cause and the source remain unclear.']],'8.C')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'Writers are often encouraged to avoid repeating words, yet repetition can preserve precision. Write an essay that argues your position on when clarity should take precedence over stylistic variety.',focus:'Use examples in which a wording choice affects what a reader can understand or do.'}),

c('primary-secondary','Primary & secondary sources',6,'evidence','3.A 4.A',
'A source’s role depends on the research question. Primary material offers direct evidence of the object or event being studied; secondary material interprets or synthesizes other evidence. Neither role alone guarantees accuracy, relevance, or fairness.',
'Identify what you are trying to learn before classifying a source. A newspaper editorial is primary evidence of its own argument but may offer secondhand claims about an event. Examine access, purpose, methods, and omissions in either case.',
'Meeting minutes can document a recorded vote. A later historian’s account can explain how that vote fits a larger pattern. The minutes may still omit discussion, and the historian’s interpretation still needs support.',
'“Primary” does not mean unbiased or automatically better. A source close to an event can be mistaken, selective, or strategically framed.','credibility citation',
p('The archive with two lenses','Investigate an event through records and later interpretations.',
'Choose a school or community event with accessible records. Assemble at least two direct records and two interpretations, or create a clearly labeled fictional dossier. Write a 600-word account and a source-role note explaining what each item can establish.',
'Build a layered timeline distinguishing when events occurred, when records were produced, and when interpretations appeared. Add an accessible source index.',
'Explain six choices of quotation, placement, or omission. Show how the same document could change source roles under a different research question.',
'Defend one contested interpretation while addressing a gap in the direct record. Revise after a peer checks whether each claim rests on a source capable of supporting it.',
'Which source seemed strongest because of its proximity to an event, and what did that proximity fail to guarantee?'),
{passage:'Original fictional research note: “For a study of why a school garden was removed, the committee’s minutes record a vote but omit the discussion. A student newspaper editorial written that week criticizes the decision as wasteful. A retrospective article five years later uses interviews to connect the removal to a larger building plan. The editorial is direct evidence of a public response; its claim about the committee’s private motives still requires corroboration.”',
read:q('The final sentence most clearly distinguishes between',[
['what a document directly demonstrates and what its claims require a researcher to verify elsewhere.','The editorial documents its own response, but it cannot alone establish another group’s private motives.'],
['a document’s official status and its usefulness for establishing any claim about the event.','The contrast is about what a source can establish, not a ranking by official status.'],
['a contemporary source that must be accurate and a later source that must be unreliable.','The note makes no blanket reliability judgment based on age.'],
['an official record that proves motives and an editorial that contains no useful evidence.','The minutes omit discussion, while the editorial remains evidence of a response.']],'3.A'),
write:q('The researcher wants to support a claim that the building plan influenced the removal decision. Which additional evidence would best serve that purpose?',[
['A dated planning memo discussed before the vote that identifies the garden site as necessary for construction.','The memo could directly connect planning considerations to the decision process.'],
['A recent photograph showing that the garden is no longer present.','The photograph confirms removal, not the reason for it.'],
['A list of positive reactions to the student newspaper editorial.','Responses to the editorial do not establish the committee’s planning considerations.'],
['An account of the garden’s popularity several years before the vote.','Earlier popularity does not demonstrate the building plan’s influence.']],'4.A')},
{type:'synthesis',prompt:'Write an essay that develops your position on the most important considerations a town should weigh when deciding how to redesign a public square.',focus:'Distinguish what firsthand perspectives, quantitative records, and interpretive proposals in the packet can each establish.'}),

c('paraphrase-summary','Paraphrase & summary',6,'evidence','3.A 4.A',
'A paraphrase restates a particular passage in new wording and syntax while preserving its meaning. A summary condenses a larger text’s central ideas. Both require attribution, and neither permits silently changing scope, certainty, or viewpoint.',
'Record the source’s claim, limits, and relationship among ideas before restating it. Then check your version against the original. Use quotation marks for wording you retain, and keep your evaluation distinct from what the source says.',
'“Some respondents preferred a trial” cannot become “Residents demanded permanent adoption.” The shift changes population, force, and duration, even if every word is different.',
'Swapping synonyms is not an adequate paraphrase. A citation acknowledges a source but does not make misleading restatement or unattributed copied language acceptable.','integrating-sources citation',
p('The compression studio','Translate one argument into three lengths without losing its limits.',
'Use a supplied source or a short openly available article. Produce a 200-word paraphrase of one section, a 100-word summary of the whole, and a 40-word public caption. Attribute each and keep a record of what was omitted.',
'Make an accordion display linking the three versions to the original claim, evidence, and qualifications. Use a text key so no relationship depends only on color.',
'Analyze six decisions about compression, syntax, attribution, or emphasis. Identify one omission that would materially change the source’s position.',
'Argue which details are indispensable for the chosen audience. Answer a reader who wants a shorter version, then revise after checking all three versions against the source.',
'At what point did compression stop preserving the source and begin replacing it with your own position?'),
{passage:'Original fictional source and student draft: Source: “In interviews with 24 residents who had attended the pilot, 15 supported repeating it next summer, provided the town added shaded seating. The interviews do not establish whether residents who stayed away would support it.” Student draft: “Most residents want the pilot to become a permanent event.” The student wants to use the source to discuss support for another trial.',
read:q('Which problem most seriously weakens the student’s use of the source?',[
['The draft broadens the population and turns conditional support for repetition into support for permanence.','It changes attendees to all residents, omits the shade condition, and changes the duration.'],
['The draft uses different words from those in the source.','Different wording is expected in a paraphrase; distortion of meaning is the problem.'],
['The source contains numerical evidence that cannot be summarized in prose.','Numerical findings can be summarized accurately with their scope and limits.'],
['The draft fails to explain why every interviewee attended the pilot.','Attendance motives are not necessary to identify the stated support and its limits.']],'3.A'),
write:q('Which revision best uses the source accurately to support discussion of another trial?',[
['According to the interview report, 15 of the 24 attendees interviewed favored repeating the pilot next summer if shaded seating were added.','The revision preserves the sample, finding, condition, and limited duration while attributing the report.'],
['The report proves that most town residents favor annual adoption after minor improvements.','The claim still generalizes beyond attendees and replaces a conditional repeat with annual adoption.'],
['The interviews show that all attendees would return if the town added shade.','Fifteen of 24 is not all, and support for repeating the event is not a promise to attend.'],
['The report suggests that shade was the only reason residents missed the pilot.','The source expressly does not establish the preferences of people who stayed away.']],'4.A')},
{type:'synthesis',prompt:'Write an essay that develops your position on how a town should balance different residents’ needs when redesigning a public square.',focus:'Paraphrase at least two sources without expanding their claims, and make clear where your reasoning begins.'}),
c('antithesis','Antithesis',7,'style','7.A 8.A',
'Antithesis places contrasting ideas in a balanced relationship, often using parallel syntax. It can clarify a distinction, sharpen a choice, or expose a tension; its persuasiveness still depends on whether the contrast is fair.',
'Name the ideas being contrasted and inspect the grammar that places them in relation. Ask what the balance highlights, what it leaves outside the comparison, and whether the argument treats a spectrum as only two alternatives.',
'“We need a room for listening, not a stage for agreement” contrasts two purposes for a public meeting. The sentence invites scrutiny of whether listening and agreement must actually conflict.',
'Balanced opposites are not evidence that only two choices exist. Antithesis can be useful without pretending that the contrast exhausts the issue.','parallelism qualification',
p('The balanced poster debate','Create a memorable contrast and then test its fairness.',
'Write two 450-word position pieces about how a school should recognize achievement. Give each a central antithetical sentence. Produce a revised version that keeps the contrast while acknowledging a meaningful overlap.',
'Design a two-panel poster plus a movable third panel for cases that resist the binary. Include equivalent text for all panels.',
'Analyze six choices of parallel structure, word order, placement, or scale. Explain how visual balance makes the opposition appear more complete than it may be.',
'Defend the distinction your poster makes while addressing a case that fits both sides. Revise the slogan and one paragraph so the exception affects the proposal.',
'What did the third panel reveal that the original balanced design encouraged readers to overlook?'),
{passage:'Original fictional editorial: “A public consultation should be a place to test a proposal, not a ceremony to celebrate it. That does not mean praise is useless. It means the meeting must make room for evidence that might change the plan. If the only permitted response is applause, the decision has already happened somewhere else.”',
read:q('The balanced contrast in the first sentence primarily',[
['distinguishes deliberation that can change a plan from participation that merely endorses it.','Testing permits revision; celebrating alone presents approval as the meeting’s purpose.'],
['claims that participants who praise a proposal cannot offer useful evidence.','The next sentence explicitly preserves a role for praise.'],
['contrasts the architectural designs of two kinds of meeting rooms.','“Place” and “ceremony” describe purposes, not building plans.'],
['suggests that public consultation should end whenever agreement is possible.','The concern is a process that excludes criticism, not agreement itself.']],'7.A'),
write:q('The writer wants to preserve a balanced contrast without implying that support and scrutiny cannot coexist. Which sentence best replaces the first sentence?',[
['A consultation should welcome support while testing its grounds, not demand support while forbidding its examination.','The parallel phrasing contrasts open and closed processes while allowing support within scrutiny.'],
['A consultation must choose between agreement and honest inquiry.','This intensifies the false opposition the writer wants to avoid.'],
['A consultation should be public, useful, scheduled, and well attended.','The list removes the relevant contrast without clarifying the purpose.'],
['A consultation can be a celebration because any approval demonstrates careful reasoning.','This equates approval with scrutiny and contradicts the passage’s concern.']],'8.A')},
{type:'analysis',packet:'gate',focus:'Examine how the speaker uses contrasts between administrative completion and visitor access without dismissing the staff’s work.'}),

c('modality-hedging','Modality & hedging',7,'style','3.C 4.C',
'Modality expresses a writer’s stance toward possibility, certainty, obligation, or permission through words such as “may,” “must,” and “should.” Hedging limits commitment when evidence or conditions do not justify an unrestricted claim.',
'Distinguish uncertainty about whether something is true from a judgment about what ought to happen. “May reduce delays” and “should reduce delays” can do different work. Choose the degree of certainty that your evidence permits.',
'“A shared calendar may reduce clashes when organizers enter events promptly” makes both the prediction and its condition visible.',
'Hedging is not adding “perhaps” to every sentence. Unsupported certainty and vague evasiveness both make an argument harder to evaluate.','qualification diction',
p('The certainty dial','Show how a change in certainty changes a public recommendation.',
'Create a small fictional pilot report with explicitly simulated results and clear limits. Write a 450-word cautious recommendation and a 450-word assertive recommendation grounded in the same report. Keep a log of every certainty or obligation marker.',
'Build a dial or graduated strip connecting claim strength to supporting evidence and conditions. Provide the full labels in a text table.',
'Analyze six changes in modality. Identify whether each expresses likelihood, duty, permission, or a limit, and explain the possible audience effect.',
'Defend the strongest wording your report supports. Address a reader who wants a definitive answer, then revise both overstatement and unnecessary hesitation.',
'Which hedge protected accuracy, and which merely concealed a claim you had not decided how to defend?'),
{passage:'Original fictional pilot report: “During two trial weeks, the shared calendar coincided with fewer room-booking conflicts. Organizers entered events voluntarily, and two large clubs did not participate. The calendar may help reduce conflicts when most organizers keep it current. The school should expand participation for another month before deciding whether to require it.”',
read:q('The phrase “may help” chiefly qualifies the report’s claim by',[
['presenting a possible contribution without asserting that the calendar alone caused the improvement.','The brief voluntary trial supports a cautious inference, especially with incomplete participation.'],
['denying that any improvement occurred during the trial.','The report states that fewer conflicts occurred.'],
['making calendar participation a mandatory obligation for every club.','The phrase concerns possible effect; a requirement remains under consideration.'],
['establishing that the calendar will work equally well in every setting.','The phrase and following condition limit, rather than universalize, the prediction.']],'3.C'),
write:q('Which revision best preserves an evidence-based limit while making the report’s recommendation more precise?',[
['The school should invite the two absent clubs into a month-long extension and compare conflicts before considering a requirement.','The revision identifies a missing group, a next step, and a decision condition.'],
['The school must require the calendar immediately because it has eliminated booking conflicts.','The trial showed fewer conflicts, not elimination or a proven cause.'],
['The school could continue gathering information without identifying which groups or outcomes the next stage should examine.','This expresses caution but leaves the study design and decision criteria less precise.'],
['The school should end the trial because incomplete participation proves the calendar ineffective.','Incomplete participation limits the evidence but does not prove ineffectiveness.']],'4.C')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'Public audiences often reward confident recommendations even when the available evidence is incomplete. Write an essay that argues your position on the responsibilities of a writer communicating uncertainty while calling for action.',focus:'Distinguish uncertainty about effects from the strength of a recommendation.'}),

c('allusion','Allusion & shared knowledge',8,'style','7.A 8.A',
'An allusion evokes another text, event, figure, or cultural story without fully retelling it. It invites readers to transfer associations to the present argument, depending on what they recognize and how they interpret that reference.',
'Explain the relevant connection rather than merely naming the source. Consider readers who recognize it differently or not at all. An allusion can compress a comparison, establish affiliation, or challenge an inherited story.',
'Calling a constantly restarted cleanup “a task for Sisyphus” evokes repeated labor without lasting progress. The comparison needs enough context to work for readers unfamiliar with the myth.',
'An allusion is not automatically authoritative evidence. Familiarity can make a comparison memorable without proving that the situations are alike.','style-voice audience-adaptation analogy-metaphor',
p('The shared-reference cabinet','Test what a cultural reference includes, compresses, and excludes.',
'Write a 500-word argument about a recurring local problem using one public-domain allusion. Create a second version for readers unfamiliar with it. Add a brief, accurately sourced explanation of the original reference.',
'Design a pair of visual cabinets: one organizes the allusion’s associations, the other shows the literal situation. Connect only the parallels you can defend; include a text key.',
'Analyze six choices in explanation, imagery, or placement. Identify where the reference might invite an inference the real situation does not support.',
'Defend whether the allusion should remain for the named audience. Address the strongest objection about exclusion or distortion, then revise after a reader attempts to explain the connection.',
'What did a reader unfamiliar with the reference notice that a knowledgeable reader might accept too quickly?'),
{passage:'Original fictional editorial: “Each Friday we clear litter from the stream; each Monday more arrives from the uncovered bins upstream. Our cleanup has begun to resemble Sisyphus pushing his stone uphill only to watch it roll back down. The comparison has a limit: our task need not last forever. Replacing the bin lids would not make cleanup unnecessary, but it could stop us from beginning at the same point every week.”',
read:q('The allusion to Sisyphus primarily helps the writer',[
['characterize the repetitive frustration of the cleanup before distinguishing the possibility of changing its cause.','The myth supplies a pattern of repeated labor; the next sentence limits the comparison.'],
['argue that any attempt to clean the stream is inherently pointless.','The proposal preserves cleanup while addressing a recurring source of litter.'],
['establish that the volunteers are responsible for creating the litter.','The passage identifies uncovered upstream bins, not volunteer misconduct.'],
['demonstrate through historical evidence that bin lids reduce pollution.','A mythic allusion is not empirical evidence about bin lids.']],'7.A'),
write:q('The writer wants to retain the allusion while making it accessible to readers unfamiliar with the myth. Which revision best achieves that goal?',[
['Like Sisyphus, condemned in the Greek myth to push uphill a stone that repeatedly rolls back down, we keep repeating work undone at its source.','The sentence supplies the relevant story and explicitly connects it to the present problem.'],
['Our cleanup is Sisyphean in the most classical sense of that expression.','The specialized adjective assumes the very knowledge the writer wants to supply.'],
['Sisyphus appears in Greek mythology alongside many other memorable figures.','The background does not explain the relevant repeated task.'],
['Because ancient stories are always true, Sisyphus proves that our cleanup will fail.','The revision misuses the allusion as proof and removes the original limitation.']],'8.A')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'Writers often rely on shared cultural references to make an argument vivid or concise. Write an essay that argues your position on the value and limitations of this practice when addressing a diverse public audience.',focus:'Consider what a reference explains, what it assumes, and what it may distort.'}),

c('understatement-hyperbole','Understatement & hyperbole',8,'style','7.A 8.A',
'Understatement deliberately represents something as less intense or significant than context suggests. Hyperbole deliberately exaggerates beyond literal accuracy. Both can shape attitude, expose a mismatch, or invite a reader to judge the gap between expression and situation.',
'Establish the literal situation before interpreting the distortion. Explain how a particular audience can recognize the gap and why it matters. Exaggeration that readers reasonably take as factual may mislead rather than create a shared rhetorical effect.',
'After a committee sends twelve reminders in an hour, calling the inbox “a little busy” understates the burden; saying it “could bury the building” exaggerates it.',
'Understatement is not simply politeness, and every false claim is not hyperbole. Context must support the intended nonliteral reading.','irony-satire tone',
p('The scale of the complaint','Represent the same inconvenience literally and nonliterally.',
'Write a 450-word literal policy complaint and a 450-word satirical column about a low-stakes routine. Use understatement and hyperbole in the column while keeping the actual problem recoverable. Label invented circumstances.',
'Make a triptych with literal, minimized, and exaggerated representations. Include a text description and identify which visual changes alter scale rather than evidence.',
'Analyze six textual and visual distortions. Explain the cues that let readers reconstruct the real complaint and identify one distortion likely to mislead.',
'Defend the version most useful for changing the policy. Address the risk that humor trivializes the problem, then revise a passage after checking what a reader believes actually happened.',
'When did a memorable exaggeration draw attention away from the action you wanted readers to consider?'),
{passage:'Original fictional satirical column: “The new study app sent twelve reminders during my first hour of homework. This was a modest contribution to the atmosphere. By the fourth reminder I had learned the most important lesson: no thought should be left uninterrupted long enough to become an idea. Perhaps the next update can finish my sentences before I begin them.”',
read:q('The phrase “a modest contribution to the atmosphere” primarily',[
['understates the interruption established by the preceding detail, creating an ironic contrast.','Twelve reminders in an hour make the restrained description conspicuously inadequate.'],
['reports a measured improvement in the room’s physical conditions.','“Atmosphere” refers to the study experience; no environmental measurement is given.'],
['concedes that the reminders were too infrequent to affect the writer.','The following sentences criticize repeated interruption.'],
['praises the app for helping the writer complete a difficult idea.','The passage instead implies that interruptions obstruct sustained thought.']],'7.A'),
write:q('The writer wants to end with hyperbole that sharpens the complaint about excessive reminders. Which sentence best achieves this goal?',[
['At this rate, the app will need to send reminders reminding its reminders to leave room for homework.','The impossible recursion exaggerates notification overload while keeping the complaint recognizable.'],
['The app sends reminders while students complete their homework.','The literal description removes the proposed exaggeration.'],
['A study has proved that every user loses half a letter grade each week.','This invents an empirical finding that readers could mistake for factual evidence.'],
['Some users may prefer a different background color for the app.','The observation shifts to an unrelated preference.']],'8.A')},
{type:'analysis',packet:'attention',focus:'Explain how recognizable exaggerations and restrained descriptions help the column question its fictional policy.'}),

c('stasis','Stasis & locating disagreement',9,'reasoning','5.A 6.A',
'Stasis is a way to locate what an argument is actually disputing. A useful set of questions concerns facts, definitions, evaluation, and action: what happened, what kind of issue it is, how it should be judged, and what should follow.',
'Map which propositions participants share and which remain contested. A new statistic may not resolve a disagreement over values, and a policy recommendation may arrive before participants agree on the problem. Use the questions flexibly rather than as a compulsory essay sequence.',
'Two readers can agree that attendance fell while disagreeing about whether that decline indicates failure and whether cancellation is justified.',
'Disagreement does not always come from missing facts. Identifying a shared factual claim can reveal a deeper disagreement about definitions, standards, or remedies.','argument definition fact-value-policy',
p('The disagreement map room','Design a deliberation that helps participants address the same question.',
'Choose a contested school decision. Write three 350-word stakeholder statements, each grounded in a common evidence dossier and a distinct, explicitly stated concern. Create a moderator’s memo locating agreements and unresolved questions.',
'Build a floor plan with rooms for factual, definitional, evaluative, and policy questions. Place statements where their disagreement belongs and provide a text route through the map.',
'Analyze six choices that either clarify the dispute or make speakers talk past one another. Explain how your arrangement affects which question seems most urgent.',
'Recommend the next question the group should investigate, using specific evidence from the statements. Address why beginning with the final policy might be attractive, then revise the moderator’s memo after a mock discussion or self-review.',
'Which apparent conflict disappeared when you separated agreement about events from disagreement about what those events mean?'),
{passage:'Original fictional committee exchange: “Both speakers accepted that attendance at the festival had fallen. One called the festival unsuccessful because it no longer drew a large crowd. The other called it successful because the smaller workshops allowed longer conversations. The chair asked for another attendance count. That count might settle the number, but the speakers already agreed on the decline; they differed about what the festival should accomplish.”',
read:q('The passage suggests that the speakers’ central disagreement concerns',[
['the criteria by which the festival’s success should be evaluated.','They accept the decline but weigh crowd size and sustained conversation differently.'],
['whether the attendance records show a decline.','The first sentence states that both speakers accept the decline.'],
['which speaker is authorized to count workshop participants.','No disagreement about counting authority appears.'],
['whether the festival occurred in the same location as last year.','The passage provides no dispute about location.']],'5.A'),
write:q('The writer wants to propose a next step that addresses the actual disagreement. Which addition best develops that reasoning?',[
['The committee should clarify whether reach, depth of participation, or a combination of the two best serves the festival’s purpose.','The proposal addresses the conflicting standards rather than recounting an accepted fact.'],
['The committee should repeat the count until both speakers accept that attendance fell.','Both already accept the decline; repetition would not resolve the evaluative dispute.'],
['The committee should interview only people who stayed away to determine whether the festival failed.','Absence may provide useful evidence about reach, but it cannot alone settle whether depth of participation also counts as success.'],
['The committee should assume that any smaller event must be less successful.','This adopts one speaker’s disputed criterion without defending it.']],'6.A')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'People sometimes respond to a disagreement by supplying more facts, even when the dispute concerns definitions or values. Write an essay that argues your position on what makes a response to disagreement productive.',focus:'Use specific cases to distinguish a lack of information from a conflict in standards or purposes.'}),

c('ethical-representation','Ethical representation',9,'evidence','3.C 4.C',
'Ethical representation gives other people’s positions and experiences enough context to be understood accurately, including qualifications that complicate the writer’s own argument. It distinguishes evidence from inference and avoids treating one voice as a substitute for an entire group.',
'Ask whose words you select, which conditions you omit, and what your framing invites readers to infer. Strong disagreement can remain fair: present the version an informed opponent could recognize before explaining its limits.',
'Quoting a resident’s support for a trial while deleting the condition that made that support possible can reverse the practical meaning of the statement.',
'Fairness does not require equal space for every claim or treating all evidence as equally sound. It requires accurate context and justified distinctions.','limitations counterargument primary-secondary paraphrase-summary',
p('The editorial responsibility desk','Publish a defensible account of a disagreement without turning people into symbols.',
'Use a supplied passage or a clearly fictional dossier containing at least three qualified positions. Write a 600-word feature and a 350-word editorial that disagrees with one position. Keep a quotation and context log; do not invent testimony from real people.',
'Design an annotated publication spread showing quote selection, caption context, and omitted material. Provide a text-only edition with the same qualifications.',
'Analyze six framing decisions across the feature and editorial. Explain who gains apparent authority or loses nuance through each choice, including the visual hierarchy.',
'Defend the editorial’s judgment against the strongest accurately represented objection. Conduct a context audit, then revise two passages where selection made the evidence seem more certain or representative than it was.',
'Which edit made your argument less convenient but more accountable to the evidence?'),
{passage:'Original fictional interview and editorial draft: A resident says, “I support the night market if the town keeps a quiet route home and reviews the noise after the trial. Without those protections, I would oppose it.” An editorial quotes only “I support the night market” and introduces the resident as evidence that nearby households have accepted the plan. The editor has interviewed no other household.',
read:q('The editorial’s treatment of the interview is most misleading because it',[
['removes conditions essential to the resident’s support and treats one interview as a broader consensus.','The quotation loses its explicit limits, while the framing expands one person’s position to households generally.'],
['uses a direct quotation instead of a paraphrase.','Direct quotation is not inherently misleading; selection and framing create the distortion.'],
['identifies a resident as someone affected by the proposal.','That context can be relevant and is not the source of the misrepresentation.'],
['describes the market as a plan before the trial has ended.','The passage does not suggest that “plan” misstates the proposal’s stage.']],'3.C'),
write:q('Which revision most accurately represents the available evidence while still acknowledging support?',[
['One interviewed resident supported a trial conditional on a quiet route home and a review of noise; the interview does not establish a neighborhood consensus.','The revision preserves the conditions and limits the population to the evidence available.'],
['Nearby households support the market, although some details may still need attention.','The sentence retains an unsupported consensus and reduces essential conditions to vague details.'],
['The resident opposed the market because noise might become a problem.','This removes the resident’s conditional support rather than representing it.'],
['The resident’s enthusiasm proves that a noise review will be unnecessary.','The source explicitly requires a review; the revision reverses that condition.']],'4.C')},
{type:'argument',prompt:'A writer must select and arrange other people’s words to make an argument, but selection can distort the positions being represented. Write an essay that argues your position on the obligations of a writer who uses individual testimony to support a public claim.',focus:'Develop criteria for preserving context, acknowledging limits, and making justified inferences.'}),
];
